<h2>What happened</h2>

<p>The petitioner held a savings account with Punjab National Bank. Requisitions arising from complaints on the national cybercrime reporting portal — from police in Maharashtra, Telangana and Karnataka — led the bank to place her account under restraint. The amount actually in dispute was Rs 2,650. The freeze, however, covered the entire account, and the bank imposed it without giving her any prior notice. She moved the Delhi High Court under Article 226.</p>

<h2>The court\'s reasoning</h2>

<p>Justice Jasmeet Singh allowed the petition. The order is short, but its logic generalises to the vast body of similar grievances.</p>

<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Serious consequences need fair process</h3><p>Freezing an account is "an action entailing serious consequences creating hardships" — it cannot be taken without natural justice and application of mind.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Proportionality</h3><p>A blanket freeze over a meagre disputed amount can cause irreparable loss that money cannot compensate; the restraint must match the dispute.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Balance of convenience</h3><p>The prima facie case and the balance of convenience favoured the account holder, not the mechanical requisition.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>The tailored order</h3><p>The account operates normally, subject to the bank\'s lien on Rs 2,650; the SOP route remains open for release of even that sum.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The wider problem of portal-driven freezes</h2>

<p>The architecture of online-fraud tracing — complaints on the cybercrime portal, money-trail mapping across beneficiary accounts, and requisitions to banks — serves a real purpose: stopping stolen funds mid-flight. But its blunt application sweeps in salary accounts and small businesses whose only connection to a fraud is an inbound credit of a few thousand rupees, sometimes received in an ordinary transaction with a stranger. Courts in several jurisdictions have been moving toward the same accommodation this order adopts: preserve the disputed amount, free the rest. The Delhi High Court\'s formulation — lien in place of lockdown — gives account holders in the capital a clear precedent to cite, and gives banks a template that protects the investigation without strangling the customer.</p>

<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Blanket freeze</th><th>Lien-limited freeze</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Entire account inoperable; salary, savings and payments blocked</td><td>Account operates normally for all other funds</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Disputed amount protected — along with everything else</td><td>Disputed amount equally protected by the lien</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Hardship disproportionate to the allegation</td><td>Restraint proportionate to the sum actually in question</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Often imposed with no notice or reasons</td><td>Requires identifying the specific amount and source requisition</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Practical guidance</h2>

<p>An account holder facing a portal-driven freeze should first obtain, in writing, the particulars: which police unit issued the requisition, under which complaint, and for what amount. The next step is a representation to the bank and the requisitioning authority seeking conversion of the blanket freeze into a lien on the disputed sum, invoking this order. Where the response is silence — the common experience — a writ petition in the High Court within whose jurisdiction the account is maintained is the effective remedy, and the relief granted here shows its realistic shape. Throughout, the account holder should preserve evidence of the account\'s ordinary use, because the contrast between years of legitimate transactions and one flagged credit is what makes the disproportion visible.</p>

<div class="note"><p>This article summarises a Delhi High Court order on bank account freezes for general information. Outcomes depend on the requisitions, amounts and conduct shown in each case. Nothing here is legal advice.</p></div>
